<DocsPage
  title="Developer reference"
  eyebrow="PUBLIC SURFACE"
  summary="Packages, contracts, state guides, and the complete generated type reference."
  alternateLocaleHref={sitePath('zh/api/')}
>
  <div class="docs-guide">
    <section class="docs-guide-callout docs-guide-callout-primary">
      <p class="docs-guide-label">API OVERVIEW</p>
      <p>
        Einfach is a spreadsheet integration system, not a DOM widget. Its APIs separate rendering, authoritative workbook
        data, and interaction state so the same grid can run on a static, Worker, remote, or product-owned backend.
      </p>
    </section>

    <h2>How the public APIs fit together</h2>
    <p>
      A normal integration crosses three layers. The UI layer renders the spreadsheet surface. The backend layer answers
      bounded projection reads and accepts edits. The state layer coordinates selection, viewport, editing, and commands
      around that surface. The UI does not own the workbook, and the backend does not manipulate the DOM.
    </p>
    <table class="docs-guide-table">
      <thead><tr><th>Surface</th><th>Responsibility</th><th>How it is used</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Solid UI binding</td><td>Renders grids, provider boundaries, and spreadsheet UI surfaces.</td><td>Mount one provider around the sheet surface after the backend is ready.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Backend contract</td><td>Owns workbook facts, computation, persistence, and bounded projection reads.</td><td>Use the bundled Worker backend or implement the contract against an existing product system.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>State APIs</td><td>Own selection, viewport, editing lifecycle, and feature-local interaction state.</td><td>Extend interaction through the provided atoms and commands rather than duplicating product state.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Type reference</td><td>Defines the exact exported types and optional capabilities.</td><td>Consult it while implementing a specific feature, not as the first integration tutorial.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <h2>Solid UI binding</h2>
    <p>
      The published Solid binding is the primary public UI surface. It provides the application-level provider, the
      virtualized grid, the Worker-backed workbook integration, and the components that form a complete spreadsheet
      shell. It is the correct starting point when the application is built with Solid and needs a production integration
      path rather than a framework experiment.
    </p>
    <p>
      Create the backend once for the lifetime of a workbook, mount the provider once above the spreadsheet area, and put
      the grid and optional shell components beneath it. The provider connects UI commands to the backend; scrolling then
      requests only the visible projection instead of expanding the complete workbook in the browser.
    </p>
    <div class="docs-guide-next">
      <a href={sitePath('docs/getting-started/')}><strong>Solid integration guide</strong><span>Installation, one complete app file, formula verification, and the next production steps. →</span></a>
    </div>

    <h2>Workbook backend contract</h2>
    <p>
      The backend contract is the boundary between the spreadsheet UI and authoritative workbook data. It reads a visible
      window or a finite range, commits cell input, and may progressively add capabilities such as import, clipboard,
      structure changes, formatting, or comments. This keeps formula calculation, permission checks, storage, and remote
      synchronization in the system that owns them.
    </p>
    <p>
      Begin with visible projection, range projection, and cell input. Add an optional method only when its matching UI
      feature is part of the product. A backend must acknowledge a write only after the authoritative system accepted it;
      that rule keeps editing feedback, history, and rendering consistent.
    </p>
    <div class="docs-guide-next">
      <a href={sitePath('docs/backend-port/')}><strong>Backend contract guide</strong><span>Required responsibilities, implementation order, capability growth, and source-backed type details. →</span></a>
    </div>

    <h2>State and interaction APIs</h2>
    <p>
      The framework-neutral UI core owns interaction state, not workbook state. Viewport APIs describe the current visible
      area. Selection APIs coordinate the active cell, ranges, pointer drag, and keyboard movement. Feature-specific APIs
      cover editing, formula input, clipboard, history, and other spreadsheet interactions without asking the host to
      build a parallel spreadsheet store.
    </p>
    <p>
      Use these APIs when adding behavior around an existing grid. Keep server data, workbook formulas, and durable product
      facts in the backend. Keep temporary interface facts such as a selection or editor lifecycle in the UI core. This is
      also the boundary that allows a backend implementation to change without rewriting the visible grid.
    </p>
    <div class="docs-guide-next">
      <a href={sitePath('docs/atoms/viewport/')}><strong>Viewport APIs</strong><span>Visible-window ownership and projection refresh behavior. →</span></a>
      <a href={sitePath('docs/atoms/selection/')}><strong>Selection APIs</strong><span>Active-cell, range, keyboard, and pointer interaction ownership. →</span></a>
      <a href={sitePath('docs/atoms/custom-formulas/')}><strong>Custom formula APIs</strong><span>Application-defined formula registration through the supported state boundary. →</span></a>
    </div>

    <h2>Framework availability</h2>
    <p>
      Solid is the published UI binding. React and Vue currently expose private repository adapters for controlled
      projections and provider hooks; they are useful for evaluating the framework boundary, but they do not yet provide a
      public npm release, bundled Worker runtime, or full spreadsheet shell. Their guides describe the verified source
      path and the exact limits of that pre-release surface.
    </p>
    <div class="docs-guide-next">
      <a href={sitePath('docs/react/')}><strong>React adapter reference</strong><span>Repository installation, verified fixture, controlled projection composition, and boundary notes. →</span></a>
      <a href={sitePath('docs/vue/')}><strong>Vue adapter reference</strong><span>Repository installation, verified fixture, controlled projection composition, and boundary notes. →</span></a>
    </div>

    <h2>Complete type reference</h2>
    <p>
      Generated TypeDoc is the exhaustive reference for exported types, properties, and optional capability signatures. Use
